# Holes

Setting: `ext_holes`.

A hole is an expression in a document, for a consumer to evaluate: the
document is a template and the hole is where a value goes, as in an
article template literal (Crichton and Krishnamurthi, "A Core Calculus
for Documents", POPL 2024). The parser records the expression as written
and never reads it. What language it is in, how it is evaluated, and
whether its value is a node, a list or an option are the consumer's.

``` example
Today I am going shopping for %{List.length items} things.
.
<p>Today I am going shopping for <code data-hole="">List.length items</code> things.</p>
```

The HTML renderer shows a hole nobody evaluated as its expression, so it
is not lost silently.

## Where a hole ends

`%{` opens a hole. Inside, every `{` must be matched by a `}`, and the `}`
that matches the opening one closes the hole.

``` example
%{ {r with x = 1} }
.
<p><code data-hole=""> {r with x = 1} </code></p>
```

A brace that should not count is escaped. `\{`, `\}` and `\\` are read as
`{`, `}` and `\`; a backslash before any other character is kept with it,
so the expression's own escapes reach the consumer as written.

``` example
%{ print_string "\}\n" }
.
<p><code data-hole=""> print_string "}\n" </code></p>
```

A hole may cross a line break, which is part of the expression. It ends
with its paragraph, heading or table cell like any inline.

``` example
a %{ f
x } b
.
<p>a <code data-hole=""> f
x </code> b</p>
```

## What is not a hole

A hole is always closed. When no closing `}` arrives before the end of the
block, or the expression is empty or all whitespace, there is no hole and
the source reads as if the `%` had been escaped.

``` example
a %{x and %{ } b
.
<p>a %{x and % b</p>
```

Without the setting, `%{.c}` is a `%` with attributes. With it, `%{` is
always a hole's opener, and `\%` writes a `%` before braces.

``` example
a %{.c} b and \%{.c} c
.
<p>a <code data-hole="">.c</code> b and <span class="c">%</span> c</p>
```

A `%` inside a code span, an autolink or a link destination is not an
opener.

``` example
`%{x}` and [a](%{u})
.
<p><code>%{x}</code> and <a href="%{u}">a</a></p>
```

## Priority

Once a hole closes, it wins over emphasis, links and spans that overlap
it: its content is not markup.

``` example
*a %{b* c}
.
<p>*a <code data-hole="">b* c</code></p>
```

Between a hole and a code span, whichever opens first wins: a code span
reads its content as text, and a hole that closes reads its own.

``` example
`a %{b` c} and %{ f "`" }
.
<p><code>a %{b</code> c} and <code data-hole=""> f "`" </code></p>
```

## Blocks

There is no block syntax. A code block whose language is `%` is a code
block to the parser; a consumer that evaluates holes may read it as one,
with its content kept exactly, blank lines and indentation included.

```` example
``` %
ul (List.map item items)
```
.
<pre><code class="language-%">ul (List.map item items)
</code></pre>
````

